---
title: "Screencasts and narrated video"
---

A `{{< video >}}` has one default and two opt-in steps up from it. The default is the
silent screencast: muted, looping, and started by the reader (hover, keyboard focus, or a
click that opens it enlarged). Nothing about it autoplays.

{{< video tour.mp4 caption="The default: a silent screencast, started by the reader." >}}

## When the reader needs to scrub

A long clip needs a scrubber. `controls` hands playback to the browser's own player, so the
reader gets play/pause, a timeline they can drag back five seconds, speed, keyboard, native
fullscreen, and picture-in-picture. The clip is still silent and still loops; the hover
preview and the click-to-enlarge stand down, because the control bar now owns those clicks.

{{< video tour.mp4 controls caption="With `controls`: the browser's own player, still silent." >}}

## When the clip is narrated

`audio` is the narrated explainer: it drops `muted` (you cannot hear a muted narration) and
`loop` (a lecture that silently restarts itself is hostile), and it implies `controls`,
because sound with no way to pause or lower it is a failure of its own. `captions=` adds a
caption track for the narration, which is why it also implies `controls`: a track that shows
by default needs a control bar to switch it off.

{{< video tour.mp4 audio captions=tour.vtt caption="With `audio` and `captions=`: a narrated explainer." >}}

Flag order is free, so the source path can come last:

{{< video controls tour.mp4 caption="The flags are not paths, so this still plays `tour.mp4`." >}}
